      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s historic library houses a room full of books and artifacts about famed lawyer Clarence Darrow, a native of Kinsman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kinsmanlibrary.org/content/township-history",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Stratton Creek, named after an early settler, winds through Kinsman's eastern section, while Pymatuning Creek rises in the northwest and passes near the old railroad bed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        6,
        11,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kinsmantownship.org/about",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Peter Allen Inn, once the home of the town doctor, now serves as a historic eatery in the former town hall building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kinsmantownship.org/about",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Kinsman sits at the crossroads of State Route 5 and State Route 7, placing it between Williamsfield and Burghill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
